WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.000
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-winning actor you know

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.300
from his work in the critically
acclaimed series "The Americans"

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.601
as well as movies like
"A Beautiful Day
in the Neighborhood."

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.000
He stars in "Perry Mason,"

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.267
which returns
for its second season

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:12.667
March 6th on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:14.734
Please welcome back to the show
our friend

00:00:14.734 --> 00:00:16.434
Matthew Rhys, everybody!

00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:18.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:25.801
♪♪

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:33.501
♪♪

00:00:33.501 --> 00:00:36.267
Well, I am so happy
you're here again.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.334
-I'm always pleased to be here.
Very happy to --

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:40.634
-You're here on a very special
day for a Welshman.

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:44.067
-It's -- Well, yes.
We're on the eve, on the cusp

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.434
of a very auspicious day.

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:48.000
Tomorrow, March the 1st,
is Saint David's Day,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.501
the feast day of
the patron saint of Wales.

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.300
A Welsh Saint Paddy's Day...
-Okay.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.367
-...as it's been told to me
a number of --

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.400
So it's basically
Welsh St. Paddy's day.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.400
Yes, so, tonight is usually
a big night for us.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.901
-Okay, gotcha. And is it a
little bit like St. Paddy's day

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:06.133
in there's some imbibing
of maybe some adult beverages?

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.367
-That's -- That's basically
all there is to do.

00:01:09.367 --> 00:01:11.200
-Okay. Gotcha.
-That's all there is.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.434
We wear red.
That's why the red socks are on.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:15.000
-Okay, wonderful.
-Yes.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.767
So there's a number of different
celebrations throughout --

00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:19.167
throughout Wales.

00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:20.968
-Are there any --
Are there any elements of it

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:23.501
that are sort of outside,
say, you know, binge drinking?

00:01:23.501 --> 00:01:25.100
-The norm? Yes. Yes.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.033
I mean, it became a tradition
with the Welsh regiment

00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:33.100
of the British Armed Forces
on March the 1st to have --

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.801
The leek is the national symbol
of Wales.

00:01:35.801 --> 00:01:37.901
-The vegetable, the leek.
-I'm sorry. Yes.

00:01:37.901 --> 00:01:39.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.667
Not when one relieves
themselves, yeah.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:45.067
So the vegetable
is an odd choice, yes,

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:47.667
but there is history behind it,
which we won't go into.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.067
So, the Welsh regiments
of the British Armed Forces

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:53.634
tend to have a leek-eating
competition on March the 1st.

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.234
-Okay.
-Which sometimes,

00:01:55.234 --> 00:01:56.801
when we do imbibe too much --

00:01:56.801 --> 00:01:58.734
and we think
this is a great idea --

00:01:58.734 --> 00:02:01.601
I partake in
that celebration myself.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:04.300
-So, now, we actually brought
a couple leeks, and now --

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.868
-Oh.
-These are --

00:02:05.868 --> 00:02:09.400
Now, I'm very upset that there's
no preparation at all.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.868
This is just a raw leek.

00:02:10.868 --> 00:02:12.601
-This is a raw --
This is a raw leek.

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:14.501
-Okay.
-I mean, this is what --

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:18.467
-So, you asked for a stopwatch.
-I did ask for a stopwatch, yes.

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:20.634
It's all been planned.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:22.634
Also, the planning
has gone hideously wrong

00:02:22.634 --> 00:02:24.701
because you have
very cleverly managed

00:02:24.701 --> 00:02:26.601
to put the small one
closest to yourself.

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:28.601
-Oh, I -- Look.
-It's like that moment in

00:02:28.601 --> 00:02:31.400
"Princess Bride" when he's like,
"You only think that happens..."

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.834
Yes.
-I'm happy to eat whatever.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:37.601
Now, I'm gonna start by saying
I'm not even a fan of leeks,

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:40.033
but how -- When you say
a leek-eating contest,

00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:44.200
like, how much leek
usually gets eaten?

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.234
-So, this being a standard,
average leek...

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:49.734
would take about an hour,
hour and a half.

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:53.467
No. It usually
does take a long time.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.200
-Yeah. Well, nobody --

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.934
I think nobody ever,
when the leek is served, thinks,

00:02:57.934 --> 00:02:59.934
"I'm gonna eat this
as fast as I can."

00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:02.200
-No, not in one sitting and raw.
-Yeah.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.501
-So I thought rather
than kind of cut together

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.534
some montage where you see us
throwing up and things,

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:09.634
we should probably
just do 30 seconds.

00:03:09.634 --> 00:03:11.267
-And see who eats the most leek.

00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:12.734
-See who gets the most
out of the leek.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:15.534
-Okay. Gotcha. Alright.
-So, hang on.

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.033
I got a tutorial on this,

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:18.534
although it's overwhelming me
already.

00:03:18.534 --> 00:03:20.167
-Now, these --

00:03:20.167 --> 00:03:22.267
-These were brought in
from Wales.

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:27.234
They're grown by my father
in his back garden, yes.

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:29.634
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you so much.

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:32.400
Nobody ever brings leeks
with back stories.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.701
-Seeing as it was
March the 1st --

00:03:34.701 --> 00:03:38.734
Now, to my mind, this is also
the first televised leek-eating,

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:41.534
you know, USA vs. Wales
leek-eating competition.

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:43.734
-Yeah.
-So I say we play --

00:03:43.734 --> 00:03:45.767
We play for the pin.
-Okay. Look at that.

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.667
It's got Wales
and the United States.

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:50.167
Here. Wait.
I'm gonna show them. Two flags.

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:52.367
This is a very exciting pin
to play for.

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:54.968
-Yeah, this is a pin
to play for.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:56.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:56.934 --> 00:04:00.434
-There's a real likelihood that
this is the start of something

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.234
and this will take off
as a televised competition.

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:03.634
[ Light laughter ]
-This is my hope.

00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:05.567
-Yeah.
-This is why I bought --

00:04:05.567 --> 00:04:08.067
I bought shares
in a leek-growing business.

00:04:08.067 --> 00:04:10.200
[ Laughter ]
In Pennsylvania.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.734
-And this is one of those things
because I've invested

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:14.334
in a lot of your things.
-Yes, you have.

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:15.701
-And none of them
have gone well.

00:04:15.701 --> 00:04:18.868
-No, but I continue to thank you
for your support.

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:20.901
Right. Hang on. Wait a second.
-Wait.

00:04:20.901 --> 00:04:23.667
I want to also note that you've
asked for a bowl of mints.

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:25.400
-I actually --
In fairness to me,

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.133
I didn't ask
for a bowl of mints.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.100
I asked for two shots
of Welsh whiskey.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.701
-Oh, well, we have that, too.
There you go.

00:04:30.701 --> 00:04:32.534
-Oh, look at that.

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:33.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:33.601 --> 00:04:35.901
-Okay.
-This is just --

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:37.267
This is turning out

00:04:37.267 --> 00:04:39.467
to be the best Saint David's Day
I've ever had.

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:41.968
-Now, I should note --
I feel very fortunate right now

00:04:41.968 --> 00:04:44.300
because my wife likes you
a great deal,

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.133
is a fan of your work,
has met you personally.

00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:47.968
And that's gonna be so helpful
when I come home

00:04:47.968 --> 00:04:50.200
with, like, leek breath.
-You can tell her it's a --

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.934
-Leek and whiskey.
-It's a Welsh tradition.

00:04:52.934 --> 00:04:55.300
Like, "Darling,
it was a Welsh tradition!"

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.300
-It does sound very made-up.
-Yes.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.300
-I'm like,
"You know St. Patrick's Day.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.934
Well, everybody has one."
-Yeah, yes!

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.100
-Okay.
-All those dirty Celts.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.467
-There's a lot of preamble
to the leek-eating.

00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:08.400
-I've been trying to put it off
hoping the stage manager

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.234
will go,
"We've run out of time."

00:05:10.234 --> 00:05:11.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:13.267
He's like, "No, we haven't."

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:15.000
-It's a very strong,
a very pungent leek.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.634
-It's the whiskey
you're smelling now.

00:05:16.634 --> 00:05:18.033
-No. It's the leek.

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:20.934
-Let's just compare leeks
quickly like we always do.

00:05:20.934 --> 00:05:22.501
Like we did
on our last birthdays.

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:25.267
-There you go. Look at that.

00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:27.367
That's why -- That's why
you and I get along so well.

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:29.067
We have similar-shaped leeks.

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:32.334
-We've compared them
over the years.

00:05:32.334 --> 00:05:33.601
-No jealousy.
-No, no.

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:35.534
-Just two guys
with very similar leeks.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:36.901
-Admiration and congratulations.

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:38.534
-Oh, I'm waving mine
the wrong way.

00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:40.501
Is it important when you --
-Oh, yes.

00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:42.667
You should always hold it
from the green.

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:44.234
Always hold it from the green.

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:45.801
And the quick tip is,
as you're doing it,

00:05:45.801 --> 00:05:47.167
you should try
and hum traditional

00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:48.834
Welsh folk songs or Welsh hymns.

00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:50.467
-Okay.
-Or if you don't know any,

00:05:50.467 --> 00:05:51.901
anything by Tom Jones
will suffice.

00:05:51.901 --> 00:05:53.200
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.133
-But humming it
will help it go down.

00:05:55.133 --> 00:05:56.601
-Just hum. Help it go down.
-Yes.

00:05:56.601 --> 00:05:59.434
-Okay, so, maybe a "three, two,
one" from the audience.

00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:02.133
[ Clears throat ]
Whenever you're ready, audience.

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:03.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:07.033
-Three, two, one! Go!

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:10.133
[ Both humming ]

00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:11.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:13.667
[ Both humming ]

00:06:18.167 --> 00:06:20.033
[ Audience chanting "Go!" ]

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:22.367
[ Both humming ]

00:06:31.934 --> 00:06:34.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:36.868 --> 00:06:38.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:45.534
-You can return the pin
to your lapel, sir.

00:06:45.534 --> 00:06:47.133
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.133 --> 00:06:49.167
-Maybe we pose this
to the audience

00:06:49.167 --> 00:06:50.701
because we didn't discuss.

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:53.434
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:56.200
Remember in "Cool Hand Luke"
when he does the eggs?

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.834
-Uh-huh.
-And at the end, they're like,

00:06:57.834 --> 00:06:59.767
"Ray, he's still
got egg in his mouth!"

00:06:59.767 --> 00:07:02.868
-Yeah.
-So, having leek --

00:07:02.868 --> 00:07:06.200
Where do we stand on having leek
in your mouth at the end?

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:09.267
-I don't know. This is --
You know, you're the expert.

00:07:09.267 --> 00:07:11.400
I feel like you should decide.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.567
-I've never done this sober.

00:07:13.567 --> 00:07:15.033
-Uh-huh.

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:17.133
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:07:17.133 --> 00:07:20.267
Yeah, I think -- Here's whiskey.

00:07:20.267 --> 00:07:23.267
[ Applause ]

00:07:23.267 --> 00:07:25.701
-It's a very different
experience sober.

00:07:25.701 --> 00:07:29.167
-Yeah.
It's a very hard vegetable

00:07:29.167 --> 00:07:31.200
to work your way through.
-It is.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.434
-Yeah, I think
you got me there, man.

00:07:33.434 --> 00:07:36.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:36.367 --> 00:07:39.467
Well, thank you.

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:41.167
-I brought two.
-You brought two?

00:07:41.167 --> 00:07:43.033
Oh, very good.

00:07:43.033 --> 00:07:45.167
-If there's one thing
the Welsh do, it's diplomacy.

00:07:45.167 --> 00:07:46.601
So bring two pins.
-Alright.

00:07:46.601 --> 00:07:49.467
Always -- That's the thing.
Always bring a second pin.

00:07:49.467 --> 00:07:51.067
-Yes. Yeah.

00:07:51.067 --> 00:07:53.834
-Now, wait.
I want to talk about your boat.

00:07:53.834 --> 00:07:55.000
-Oh, God.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.200
-You've got a boat
called Rarebit.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.567
-Another Welsh-themed question.
Yes, I do.

00:07:59.567 --> 00:08:01.334
-Now, here's the thing
about "Rarebit."

00:08:01.334 --> 00:08:03.501
This was the name of this boat
before you bought it.

00:08:03.501 --> 00:08:07.968
-In 1939, this boat was built
in Brooklyn and named "Rarebit."

00:08:07.968 --> 00:08:10.901
So when I saw it on eBay
one drunken night,

00:08:10.901 --> 00:08:13.467
I went, "Well, this is fate.
I live in Brooklyn."

00:08:13.467 --> 00:08:16.300
-So you just got a mouthful
of leek and you're on eBay.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.033
-Had a Welsh whiskey.
Keri was asleep.

00:08:18.033 --> 00:08:21.400
I was like, "Buy."

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:22.968
I had all these giddy, dizzy,

00:08:22.968 --> 00:08:25.400
rose-tinted plans
for this thing.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.300
-Now, you do have this boat,
and you take people out.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:30.100
You are the captain
of this vessel.

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:31.467
-Sadly, I'm not the captain.

00:08:31.467 --> 00:08:32.801
I have a great captain,
Captain Kelly.

00:08:32.801 --> 00:08:34.734
So, yes, we now charter it

00:08:34.734 --> 00:08:36.400
because the restoration
took so long.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.701
In the end,
myself and Captain Kelly,

00:08:38.701 --> 00:08:40.567
we did the restoration.

00:08:40.567 --> 00:08:42.968
We -- They call it
splining the hull.

00:08:42.968 --> 00:08:45.200
We splined the hull.
We glassed it and everything.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.601
So we now take six paying,
sane people out

00:08:49.601 --> 00:08:52.334
in New York Harbor on a charter.

00:08:52.334 --> 00:08:54.701
-And, now, what percentage
of them know you

00:08:54.701 --> 00:08:56.400
as Matthew Rhys the actor

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:59.234
and what percentage just think
you're a weird guy with a boat?

00:08:59.234 --> 00:09:00.868
[ Laughter ]
-I think 99% of people

00:09:00.868 --> 00:09:03.067
think I'm the weird guy
with the boat when I'm like,

00:09:03.067 --> 00:09:06.334
"Argh! Welcome to the boat!"
And they're like, what?

00:09:06.334 --> 00:09:08.634
It's like, "Stop trying
to be Quint in 'Jaws.'"

00:09:08.634 --> 00:09:10.534
And I'm like, "Alright."

00:09:10.534 --> 00:09:13.033
But, yeah, sometimes
if the deckhand can't do it,

00:09:13.033 --> 00:09:16.133
I go in and I kind of pretend
that I know what I'm doing.

00:09:16.133 --> 00:09:17.400
-Alright, I have more questions

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:18.767
about your boat
and "Perry Mason."

00:09:18.767 --> 00:09:20.634
We'll be right back with more
from Matthew Rhys.

